# Target C: effective small-slack chart reconstruction for an arbitrary unit wall

Date: 2026-10-07. Authority: audit.75, main
`baadff26b3fae1107f8ed2d9d18f03f78cd4a23b`.
**Proved candidate awaiting independent audit.** No promotion.

## 1. Scope, implication and missing transfer

Let F be any convex polygon with b cyclic unit sides in a unit-separated
set X consisting of those b wall vertices and z interior points. Let
n=b+z, a=sqrt(3)/4, and define the area-form Oler slack

`sigma=area(F)-a*(2*z+b-2)`.

Suppose 0<=sigma<=1/1000. Then X has a triangulation combinatorially
equal to the full unit triangular lattice in a convex lattice polygon:
all interior valences are six, and boundary vertices have one, two or
three incident triangles. The developed boundary has at most six
nonstraight corners, with turns multiples of pi/3. After matching this
chart and anchoring a boundary unit edge, every actual point differs
from its developed lattice point by at most 60*b*sigma. Every
triangulation edge has length between 1 and 1+5*sigma_T, where sigma_T
is its adjacent triangle's area excess above a.

This is a genuine arbitrary-unit-wall conclusion at an explicit small
**total** slack scope. It is not an arbitrary one-deficit classification:
one point less adds sqrt(3)/2 to this area-form slack, far beyond the
hypothesis. Canonical macroscopic chambers have only sigma=O(D), not
sigma<=1/1000. The missing transfer is precisely the reduction of that
boundary-order slack to a usable chart/packet. No area-to-capacity
inference is made.

For the arithmetic six-direction wall with z=N and
`0<theta<=1/(10000*M^3)`, the earlier explicit area bound gives
sigma<1/1000. This theorem supplies the complete chart **uniformly in M**,
with displacement at most 1000*M^4*theta. It replaces a nonquantitative
Oler-limit localization in this joint regime. Finite triangle forcing
still needs the quantitative rate/energy and isolation bridge.

`small total full-wall Oler slack -> exact triangular combinatorics and
explicit chart displacement -> bounded approximate rate system ->
uniform finite triangle or controlled defect packet`.

Only the first two implications are proved here. They advance the
maximum-population uniformity obligation and identify the exact slack
loss that an arbitrary-container transfer must overcome.

## 2. Saturation supplies a bounded-radius triangulation

Oler for a convex unit wall states
`n<=area(F)/(sqrt(3)/2)+b/2+1`.
Its right side is n+sigma/(sqrt(3)/2). Since sigma<sqrt(3)/2,
no further unit-separated point can be inserted in the closed F.

Extend X to an auxiliary separated packing of the whole plane by
enumerating a dense countable set and successively adding a point when
its distance from the current set is at least one. The packing is locally
finite. Its covering radius is at most one: a point at distance greater
than one would have a dense proposed neighbour that either was accepted
or was blocked by an even closer accepted point. No added point lies
in F by the preceding integer Oler bound. This auxiliary construction
is not a source competitor and uses no diameter upper constraint.

The Voronoi cells are bounded by the covering-radius property. Their
planar dual has convex cyclic Delaunay faces; triangulate co-circular
faces arbitrarily. Each empty circumcircle has radius at most one,
since its center has distance at most one from the packing. Every unit
wall edge belongs to this dual: its midpoint has distance 1/2 from its
two endpoints, and every other separated packing point has midpoint
distance at least sqrt(3)/2. A neighbourhood on the perpendicular
bisector therefore belongs only to those two Voronoi cells. Thus all
wall edges survive in the triangulation.

Restrict to the triangles enclosed by the wall cycle. Their vertices
are exactly X. Euler gives T=2*z+b-2 triangles. The triangulation is
Delaunay, so opposite angles at every internal edge sum to at most pi.
All its edges have length at least one and every triangle has
circumradius at most one. Degenerate co-circular cases do not affect
the nondegenerate triangle areas or the opposite-angle inequality.

## 3. Triangle area and an obtuse-pair gap

For a triangle with sides at least one and circumradius R<=1, every
angle is at least l=asin(1/(2R))>=pi/6, and R>=1/sqrt(3). The area is
2*R^2 times the product of its three angle sines. Concavity of log(sin)
on the angle simplex shows its minimum at fixed R occurs at angles
l,l,pi-2l. The minimum area is

`sqrt(4*R^2-1)/(4*R^2)>=sqrt(3)/4=a`.

The last inequality follows by differentiating its square on
R^2 in [1/3,1]: its maximum is at 1/2 and its endpoint minima agree.
Thus every triangle has nonnegative excess sigma_T=area(T)-a and
sum sigma_T=sigma.

Suppose a triangle has largest angle alpha>=pi/2. Then alpha<=2pi/3,
and its opposite edge length L satisfies
L^2>=2-2*cos(alpha)>=2. This edge is internal, since every boundary
edge has length one. Its neighbour has opposite angle beta<=pi-alpha
<=pi/2. Its other two angles are at least pi/6. Concavity of log(sin)
with their fixed sum bounds their product below by
(1/2)*sin(beta+pi/6). Its area is consequently at least

`L^2/(4*sin(beta))*sin(beta+pi/6)`
`=L^2/4*(sqrt(3)/2+(1/2)*cot(beta))`
`>=sqrt(3)*L^2/8`.

The first triangle has area at least (1/2)*sin(alpha). The pair's
total area is at least

`(1/2)*sin(alpha)+(sqrt(3)/4)*(1-cos(alpha))`.

This function is increasing for alpha in [pi/2,2pi/3], so it is at
least (2+sqrt(3))/4. Subtracting the two baseline areas gives the
fixed gap (2-sqrt(3))/4>1/1000. This contradicts the total slack.
Hence **every triangle is acute**. The gap is a valid lower bound;
sharpness is not asserted.

## 4. Quantitative edge and angle control

Let L be an acute triangle's largest side. If L>=sqrt(2), its area
is at least 1/2. For completeness, put x,y for the other squared
side lengths. They lie in [1,L^2], and acuteness gives x+y>=L^2.
Heron's squared area
`[4*x*y-(x+y-L^2)^2]/16` is concave in (x,y). Its minimum on that
polygon is attained at a vertex; evaluating the vertices gives at
least (L^2-1)/4>=1/4. Thus L>=sqrt(2) contradicts
area<=a+sigma<1/2.

For L<sqrt(2), projecting the opposite vertex onto its base shows
its height is at least sqrt(1-L^2/4): both other sides are at least
one and the projection is within the segment. Hence its area is at
least f(L)=L*sqrt(4-L^2)/4. The function is increasing up to sqrt(2),
and f(11/10)>a+1/1000. Therefore L<11/10. On [1,11/10],
f'(L)=(2-L^2)/(2*sqrt(4-L^2))>1/5. It follows that

`L-1<=5*sigma_T`.

All three sides are in [1,11/10]. Differentiating the cosine law
bounds each partial derivative of cos(angle) by two. Throughout this
cube the angle sine exceeds 4/5. Therefore

`|angle-pi/3| <= (5/2)*sum(side-1) <= 50*sigma_T`.

These are exact analytic bounds, not empirical near-equilateral tests.

## 5. Valences and global equilateral development

At an interior vertex k incident triangle angles sum to 2pi. Their
total deviation from k*pi/3 is at most 50*sigma<=1/20<pi/3.
Thus k=6. At a boundary vertex the angle sum is its convex interior
angle, at most pi. The same bound implies k<=3; k>=1 since the
triangulation is nondegenerate. Each developed boundary turn is
(3-k)*pi/3, so it is nonnegative. The discrete angle sum/Euler identity
gives total boundary turn 2pi; there are at most six nonzero turns.

Replace each triangle by a unit equilateral one and develop across
shared edges. Development around an interior vertex closes exactly
because it has six triangles. Since the complex is a topological disk,
these vertex circuits generate its compatibility relations: development
is single valued. Its boundary is a closed convex polygon with unit
lattice edges and turns 0,pi/3 or 2pi/3. The local orientation is
positive and the convex boundary has degree one, so the development
is one-to-one and covers that polygon. Its vertices are precisely all
triangular-lattice sites in it; any missing site would lie inside a
unit elementary triangle or edge and cannot be a distinct lattice site.

Anchor one boundary edge to its actual edge. Along a simple dual path,
edge directions differ from their developed directions by at most
50*sum_path sigma_T<=50*sigma. Each edge length differs from one by
at most 5*sigma. Hence corresponding edge vectors differ by at most
55*sigma. A convex triangular-lattice polygon is the intersection of
interval bounds on i, j and i+j. A two-direction shortest lattice path
can keep each of these three coordinates between its endpoint values:
use u,v when the coordinate changes have the same sign, and otherwise
use the diagonal (1,-1) or (-1,1) plus the remaining axis direction.
Every intermediate site therefore stays in the polygon. Its length is
max(|delta_i|,|delta_j|,|delta_i+delta_j|)<=b, since each coordinate's
boundary range is at most b/2. Summing edge errors gives 60*b*sigma.
The spare constant covers the choice of path endpoint/anchor.

For the original regular-hexagon development this path length is at
most 2M. The first actual wall edge differs in angle from its prescribed
flat direction by at most (M-1)*theta, and its corner anchor shift is
at most M^2*theta. The parallel-body bound gives sigma<=7*M^3*theta.
Thus displacement from the fixed P_M chart is at most

`M^2*theta+2*M*((M-1)*theta+55*sigma)`
`<=1000*M^4*theta`.

At theta<=1/(10000*M^3), sigma<=6/10000+pi/(10^8*M^2)<1/1000.
The six boundary corner valences are two and the remaining boundary
valences three, since their actual angle deviations from 2pi/3 or pi
are less than 2*(M-1)*theta and the total triangle error is below 1/20.
So the developed polygon is exactly H_M, proving the uniform chart claim.

## 6. Exact limits and continuation

This theorem neither discards isolates nor promotes auxiliary points
to KKT-positive. Saturation points live outside F only for proving
triangulation inequalities; they are not inserted into a source endpoint.
The effective localization uses the full wall-plus-cloud Oler count.

It does not classify deficit-one clouds, prove a general small-slack
triangle, supply additional capacity, or treat a nonunit cap closing
chord as a unit wall. Macroscopic canonical chamber slack O(D) is the
explicit missing hypothesis. A next attack is to charge triangles with
large defects to that slack and couple the remaining lattice regions
without boundary-order loss; another is to use the explicit chart bound
in a quantitative finite sorting isolation proof for growing M.

The exact companion verifier checks elementary-triangle areas, acute
angles, bounded circumradii, every internal Delaunay edge, complete
pair separation and the explicit displacement bound in five rational
sorting examples. It also checks the exact constants of the proof.
Those finite checks are corroboration, not the arbitrary-wall theorem.
